{{!--
	skin.mustache -- the Blitzy skin's root template, and the only entry point into
	includes/templates/.

	MediaWiki\Skin\SkinMustache::generateHTML() renders the template named by the `template`
	argument of ValidSkinNames.blitzy, falling back to `skin`, resolved against the directory
	named by `templateDirectory` (includes/Skin/SkinMustache.php L46-53). skin.json sets those
	two to "skin" and "includes/templates", so this file's name is part of the manifest
	contract rather than a convention: rename it and the skin stops rendering entirely.
	Partials resolve by filename the same way, and TemplateParser's resolver throws "Could not
	find partial" rather than rendering a blank when a name matches no file
	(includes/Html/TemplateParser.php L217-226). Every reference below is therefore spelled
	exactly as its file is, PascalCase included.

	This block comment uses the `--` delimited form. LightnCandy strips that form with a
	single non-greedy pattern before it tokenises anything (SafeString::EXTENDED_COMMENT_SEARCH,
	applied from Validator::verify(), which Compiler::compileTemplate() calls first), so a
	delimited comment may quote a template expression. The plain form cannot: it ends at the
	first closing double brace it meets, which truncates the comment and compiles the rest of
	the prose as markup. Bundled Vector uses the delimited form in Logo.mustache for exactly
	this reason. The one sequence this comment must never contain is the delimited form's own
	terminator.

	================================================================================
	INVOCATION GRAPH -- the whole reason this file exists (Gate 9)

	    skin.mustache
	      |-- {{#data-blitzy-announcement}} {{>AnnouncementBar}} {{/data-blitzy-announcement}}
	      |-- {{>NavCard}}                            ---> {{>PersonalMenu}}
	      |-- {{>ContentHeader}}
	      |-- {{#is-blitzy-toc-available}} {{#data-toc}} {{>TableOfContents}} ... ---> itself
	      |-- {{>ContentBody}}                        ---> {{>CategoryFooter}}
	      `-- {{>SiteFooter}}

	Six references leave this file. PersonalMenu is reached through NavCard, CategoryFooter
	through ContentBody, and TableOfContents recurses into itself for every nesting level
	below the first -- which is sanctioned rather than tolerated, because
	SkinMustache::getTemplateParser() calls enableRecursivePartials( true ) and annotates it
	"For table of contents rendering." (includes/Skin/SkinMustache.php L31-38). Nine files in
	this directory, nine reachable partials, no orphan and no tenth file (R12). Gate 9 counts
	an unreachable partial as a wiring failure even when the orphaned file is perfect in
	isolation, so adding a partial without adding it to the graph above breaks the gate.

	No partial is referenced twice. Each one puts at least one fixed id on the page: its own
	in six of them, and one of core's -- #firstHeading with the portlet ids, or #catlinks --
	by way of the markup ContentHeader and CategoryFooter pass through. A second reference
	would therefore duplicate ids page-wide, which is a blocking R10 failure across all
	twenty-one captures rather than a local defect.

	Landmarks follow from that arithmetic: exactly one <header> (NavCard), one <main>
	(ContentBody), one <footer> (SiteFooter) and one <h1> (core's #firstHeading, arriving
	through ContentHeader). Because each landmark is unique, none of the three needs an
	accessible name; the several <nav> elements inside the partials are named there.

	================================================================================
	TEMPLATE DATA CONSUMED -- four keys, and nothing else

	One thing about the skip link is worth knowing before writing an assertion against it.
	It is the first element THIS FILE emits, but not the first element inside the rendered
	body: OutputPage::headElement() appends an empty div#mw-aria-live-region[aria-live=polite]
	immediately after the body start tag (includes/Output/OutputPage.php L3755-3764), before
	any skin markup, and no Mustache skin can reach it. Measured on a rendered page. So the
	precise contract is "first skin-emitted element, immediately after core's ARIA live
	region" -- a `body > *:first-child` assertion would fail here and in every other skin
	alike. The affordance is unaffected: core's container is empty and not focusable, so the
	skip link is still the first thing a keyboard user reaches.

	  string   msg-blitzy-jumptocontent
	           The skip link's text. SkinMustache::getTemplateData() creates every msg- key
	           from the `messages` argument of skin.json through Message::text(), so it
	           arrives as plain text and is interpolated escaped. This is the only message
	           key this file references and the only new key it needs documented in
	           i18n/qqq.json for the banana checker (Gate 2).

	           It is `blitzy-jumptocontent` and not `jumptocontent` because core has no
	           `jumptocontent` message -- verified directly against
	           mediawiki/languages/i18n/en.json, which offers `jumpto`, `jumptonavigation`
	           and `jumptosearch` and nothing that reads "jump to content". Vector likewise
	           defines its own `vector-jumptocontent`.

	  object   data-blitzy-announcement
	           MediaWiki\Skins\Blitzy\BlitzyViewModel's announcement payload, used here ONLY
	           as a section key. The view model omits the key altogether when the bar is
	           disabled or has no text, so the section is the enable gate and AnnouncementBar
	           is entered with the payload as its context, which is what lets that file read
	           `text`, `label` and `href` unqualified.

	  string|null html-site-notice
	           Skin::getSiteNotice() (includes/Skin/Skin.php L1918-1962), or null when there
	           is nothing to show -- SkinMustache passes it through `?: null`. Finished HTML
	           built by core, so it is interpolated raw; that is one of the reserved lawful
	           uses of an unescaped tag, and escaping it would print core's markup as visible
	           text. MediaWiki:Sitenotice content arrives inside div#localNotice[data-nosnippet]
	           and CentralNotice injects its banners through the same key.

	  bool     is-blitzy-toc-available
	           BlitzyViewModel's flag, true only when core reported at least one section.
	           Used here ONLY as a section key.

	`data-toc` is entered as a context rather than read as a value; see the ruling below.
	No other key is touched. In particular no configuration value is interpolated anywhere in
	this file, which is why R13's escaping obligation lives entirely in AnnouncementBar.mustache
	and NavCard.mustache and nothing here can become an injection site.

	================================================================================
	TWO PLACEMENTS THAT ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT COSMETIC

	1. TableOfContents and ContentBody are SIBLINGS inside one div.blitzy-content-layout,
	   with the table of contents first in source order.

	   That order is what lets components/TableOfContents.less stick the card beside the
	   content at 1024px and up and reorder it below the content at narrower widths from ONE
	   DOM instance. Nesting it inside ContentBody, or including it from
	   ContentBody.mustache, makes the sticky-then-reorder behaviour unimplementable without
	   a second copy of the list -- and a second copy duplicates every blitzy-toc-<anchor>
	   id, which is a blocking R10 failure. The markup is identical at every width; only the
	   stylesheet changes, which is also what keeps the two presentations byte-identical
	   inputs to the capture pass (R9).

	   Being first in source order is not a statement about which side it sits on. Which side
	   is flip-aware stylesheet work under R6, so nothing here encodes a direction.

	2. AnnouncementBar is invoked inside a section on the announcement OBJECT, never behind
	   an is- boolean.

	   A falsy section renders nothing at all, so a disabled bar is ABSENT from the document's
	   flow rather than hidden: `display: none` is explicitly non-compliant, and the assertion
	   is made against the HTML source, not against visibility. Together with
	   is-blitzy-toc-available these are the only two conditionals in this file, and they are
	   the specification's only sanctioned conditional absences. A third one would put a
	   fidelity assertion at risk of failing against an element that was never rendered
	   rather than reporting a difference (R11), so do not add one.

	================================================================================
	RULING -- WHY THE TABLE OF CONTENTS IS ENTERED THROUGH {{#data-toc}}

	TableOfContents.mustache states an invocation contract: it must be entered with the
	`data-toc` object as its context. Its root branch is {{^anchor}} and its leaf branch is
	{{^array-sections}}, and `array-sections` lives INSIDE `data-toc`. Entered at the top
	level, the list renders empty and the leaf branch also fires, emitting one stray <li> with
	an empty id and href="#" -- an anchor with no accessible name, which the blocking
	accessibility gate rejects (R10). That file records the extra <li> as measured rather than
	assumed, and MediaWiki compiles with LightnCandy's FLAG_MUSTACHELOOKUP but not
	FLAG_PARENT, so no parent path could rescue it from the wrong context.

	The push adds no gate. Core's SkinComponentTableOfContents returns an empty array for a
	page with no table of contents, for __NOTOC__ and for a parse with no sections, and
	otherwise sets number-section-count above zero; BlitzyViewModel derives
	is-blitzy-toc-available from that same count. So `data-toc` is non-empty exactly when the
	flag is true, and the inner section only changes the context -- it never suppresses
	anything the flag permitted. Inside it, msg-toc and msg-blitzy-toc-toggle still resolve,
	because FLAG_MUSTACHELOOKUP walks up the context stack for a key the current context does
	not hold.

	================================================================================
	WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT EMIT, AND WHAT IT WILL NOT

	A Mustache skin templates the content of the body element and nothing outside it, so this
	file emits no html, head, body, title, meta or link element -- and has no way to reach one
	even if it tried. Anything reaching further has a seam of its own:

	  the theme class    Hooks\BlitzyHooks applies skin-theme-clientpref-night / -day / -os
	                     to the root html element through OutputPage::addHtmlClasses()
	                     (includes/Output/OutputPage.php L736-738). Emitting a theme class
	                     here would be a silent no-op that an isolated template test cannot
	                     catch, which is why Gate 13 asserts it through a real page render.
	  body classes       skin.json's `bodyClasses` argument supplies skin-blitzy.
	  stylesheets        the ResourceModules declared in skin.json, delivered by
	  and scripts        ResourceLoader. No script element, no inline event-handler attribute
	                     and no subresource of any kind is emitted here: no image, icon,
	                     font, preload or preconnect, so this file cannot originate the
	                     foreign-origin request the external-origins guard fails on (R5).
	  colours and        every value resolves in layouts/screen.less against tokens.less.
	  measures           No style attribute and no colour literal appears below (R3).

	Nothing here is generated, random, timed or request-dependent, and the DOM order below
	never varies, which is what makes two renders of one revision byte-identical (R9). There
	is no positive tabindex, no aria-* attribute that native semantics already carry, and
	nothing that needs the client script to work: with scripting disabled this skeleton is
	complete, and every disclosure inside the partials is core's documented checkbox hack
	(R4).

	================================================================================
	CLASS HOOKS DEFINED HERE -- the contract with layouts/screen.less

	  .mw-jump-link          core's own class, kept verbatim (R2). Core styles it in no skin
	                         feature -- accessibility.less covers reduced motion and image
	                         sizing only -- so the skin's stylesheet owes it the treatment
	                         its message documentation promises: visually hidden until it
	                         takes keyboard focus, never display: none, which would take it
	                         out of the focus order and defeat the affordance.
	  .blitzy-page           the full-width page surface. Background and vertical rhythm.
	  .blitzy-page__inner    the content measure and its gutters, centred with auto inline
	                         margins. Two elements rather than one so that a full-bleed
	                         surface and a constrained measure are each assertable on their
	                         own element.
	  .blitzy-site-notice    the site-notice band. A class and no id: core owns
	                         div#siteNotice, and it wraps the notice in one itself when a
	                         skin sets the `wrapSiteNotice` option, so emitting that id here
	                         would risk a duplicate rather than add a hook.
	  .blitzy-content-layout the two-column relationship between the table of contents and
	                         the content body, and the single place their responsive
	                         behaviour is authored.

	The announcement band and the navigation card sit OUTSIDE .blitzy-page on purpose: the
	band is full-bleed and the card is inset from the viewport edge, so both are positioned
	against the viewport rather than against the content measure.

	================================================================================
	DELIBERATELY ABSENT (R12)

	No sticky header, no breadcrumb bar, no back-to-top control, no cookie banner, no
	reading-progress indicator, no invented sidebar and no search field of this file's own --
	the navigation card owns the search box. Bundled Vector's forty-eight templates, its
	Footer__row sub-parts and its Dropdown/ subdirectory show what MediaWiki permits; R12
	fixes what this skin ships, which is nine flat files and the regions below.

	Rendered output, in full: the skip link; then, when configured, the announcement band;
	then the navigation card; then div.blitzy-page wrapping div.blitzy-page__inner, which
	holds the optional site-notice band, the content header, div.blitzy-content-layout with
	the table of contents and the content body as siblings, and the site footer.
--}}
<a class="mw-jump-link" href="#bodyContent">{{msg-blitzy-jumptocontent}}</a>
{{#data-blitzy-announcement}}
{{>AnnouncementBar}}
{{/data-blitzy-announcement}}
{{>NavCard}}
<div class="blitzy-page">
	<div class="blitzy-page__inner">
		{{#html-site-notice}}
		<div class="blitzy-site-notice">{{{.}}}</div>
		{{/html-site-notice}}
		{{>ContentHeader}}
		<div class="blitzy-content-layout">
			{{#is-blitzy-toc-available}}
			{{#data-toc}}
			{{>TableOfContents}}
			{{/data-toc}}
			{{/is-blitzy-toc-available}}
			{{>ContentBody}}
		</div>
		{{>SiteFooter}}
	</div>
</div>
